<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  IIS does not read .htaccess at all, so tools/.htaccess protects nothing on a
  Windows/IIS install. This file is the IIS equivalent: deny every request that
  maps into tools/.

  tools/ holds command-line maintenance scripts — installers, schema repair,
  backup/restore, audits, token minting. Every script here also carries a
  PHP_SAPI guard; this is the server-level half of the same fence.

  ── ONE MECHANISM: REQUEST FILTERING ─────────────────────────────────

  IIS offers two ways to deny a directory, and TicketsCAD uses exactly one of
  them everywhere: this file, sql/web.config, and the file
  backup_harden_dir() (inc/backup.php) writes into the backup directory all
  carry the same four lines. Two shapes in one tree is how one of them rots.

  The one used is Request Filtering. URL Authorization
  (<security><authorization><add accessType="Deny" users="*" />) was rejected:

    1. Request Filtering is part of the DEFAULT IIS feature set. URL
       Authorization is an optional role service. A web.config naming a
       section whose module is absent does not degrade to something safe —
       IIS answers HTTP 500.19 for the whole directory. That is a deny by
       accident, it is the exact bug recorded below, and it is what makes an
       administrator delete the file and restore the exposure.
    2. The reporter measured a clean 401 from URL Authorization — but only
       after running DISM to install the URL Authorization feature; he later
       concluded it had been present all along. What is verified is that the
       500 failures reproduce on an untouched host. What is NOT verified is a
       401 on a host where URL Authorization was never installed. A competent
       administrator investigating his own machine first concluded the feature
       was missing, so its presence is not self-evident either way.
    3. Request Filtering denies FILES. The report that started this was
       /backups/ answering 403 while the archive inside it answered 200 and
       served a complete database export. A rule that denies the listing and
       not the file is not a fix.

  URL Authorization remains available as an OPTIONAL extra layer for an
  administrator who knows the role service is installed — see
  docs/WEB-SERVER-HARDENING.md. It is never the only rule in a shipped file.

  ── WHAT THIS DENIES, EXACTLY ────────────────────────────────────────

  allowUnlisted="false" with nothing listed as allowed means every request
  whose URL carries a file name extension is refused: .php, .sql, .zip, .bak,
  anything. Denied requests get HTTP 404 with substatus 404.7, "File Extension
  Denied" — documented at
  learn.microsoft.com/iis/configuration/system.webserver/security/requestfiltering/fileextensions/
  The client sees a plain 404; the substatus is what lands in the IIS log.

  Extension-less URLs — GET /tools/, GET /tools/upgrade/ — are refused too, and
  that is the part worth knowing rather than assuming. With
  allowUnlisted="false" IIS treats "no extension" as unlisted and answers
  404.7 for those as well; it is why extension-less applications (ASP.NET MVC)
  must add <add fileExtension="." allowed="true" /> before they work at all.
  Microsoft's archived note on the subject is titled "Getting 404.7 error for
  '/' root requests after Disabling Allow Unlisted file extension". So do NOT
  add a "." entry to this file — it would reopen every directory URL.

  Request filtering runs at the start of the pipeline, before the file is
  looked up, so a request for a path that does not exist under tools/ is
  refused the same way rather than reaching the 404 handler. Nothing in the
  response distinguishes a real script from an imaginary one.

  Two limits, stated rather than papered over:

    * INHERITANCE. If a site- or server-level configuration already sets
      allowUnlisted="false" AND allow-lists extensions, those allow entries are
      inherited here and an allow-listed extension would still be served out of
      this directory. Stock IIS ships allowUnlisted="true" with every listed
      extension denied, so nothing is inherited as allowed out of the box. If
      your server is the hardened case, add <clear /> as the first child of
      <fileExtensions> below.
    * This file is not the last line of defence and is not meant to be. Every
      script under tools/ refuses to run under a non-CLI SAPI and answers
      "403 CLI only" before touching the database — that one works on any
      server, in any configuration, including none of this.

  The behaviour above is read off Microsoft's documentation; it has not been
  measured by this project against a live IIS. <directoryBrowse enabled="false" />
  stays regardless: it is a second, independent stop for the case that would
  otherwise leak a file list.

  ── DO NOT REWRITE THIS AS <authorization> ───────────────────────────

  The first version of this file shipped in v4.2.3 and returned HTTP 500.19 on
  every stock IIS install instead of denying. Access was blocked, but only
  because the file was malformed enough to throw — not because the rule worked.
  A tester on stock IIS 10 / Windows 11 took the three defects apart one at a
  time, each fatal on its own:

    1. <authorization> was a direct child of <system.webServer>. IIS URL
       Authorization lives under <security>. Misplaced → 500.19.
    2. <deny users="*" /> is the ASP.NET element (system.web/authorization).
       IIS URL Authorization uses a different schema entirely:
       <add accessType="Deny" users="*" />. Same-sounding name, wrong element.
    3. applicationHost.config ships <add accessType="Allow" users="*" /> at
       server level and the collection is keyed on `users`, so adding a second
       users="*" entry collides — 0x800700b7, "cannot add duplicate collection
       entry". The <remove users="*" /> MUST come first. This is a property of
       the DEFAULT IIS configuration, so it failed on every stock install.

  Those three are kept on the record because they are what a hand-written
  <authorization> block hits again, not because this file uses one.

  ── WHY THERE IS NO hiddenSegments RULE ──────────────────────────────

  v4.2.3 also carried <hiddenSegments><add segment="." /></hiddenSegments>.
  That returned HTTP 200 — it neither errored nor blocked, so both mechanisms
  in the shipped file were inert. It is gone rather than repaired, for two
  reasons. It is the wrong tool: the file-extension rule above denies this
  directory and everything under it. And it is a dangerous tool: hiddenSegments
  matches ANY path segment, not just the first, so a segment named for a
  directory blocks every nested path that happens to share the name. The same
  reporter proved that by adding segment="vendor" at site level, which blocked
  assets/vendor/bootstrap and left every page unstyled. This project has
  already unstyled two live sites with an over-broad vendor rule once. A
  mechanism that implies protection while returning 200 is worse than no
  mechanism at all.

  Reported and diagnosed by Ron Jones (@rjonesbsink).
-->
<configuration>
  <system.webServer>
    <security>
      <requestFiltering allowDoubleEscaping="false">
        <fileExtensions allowUnlisted="false" />
      </requestFiltering>
    </security>
    <directoryBrowse enabled="false" />
  </system.webServer>
</configuration>
